u-parRa", "headword": "सु-पर्ण", "headword_slp1": "su-parRa", "fold": "su-parRa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "1. Su-parṇa, ‘well-winged,’ designates a large bird of prey, the ‘eagle’ or the ‘vulture,’ in the Rigveda and later. In the passages in which it appears as an eater of carrion it must be the vulture. The Jaiminīya brāhmaṇa mentions an eagle which separates milk from water like the kruñc. In the Rigveda the suparṇa is said to be the child of the śyena, and is distinguished from the latter in another passage: this led Zimmer to think that the falcon is probably meant. The Atharvaveda alludes to its cry, and describes it as living in the hills.\nFoot notes i. 164, 20; ii. 42, 2; iv. 26, 4; viii. 100, 8; ix. 48, 3, etc.\nAv. i. 24, 1; ii. 27. 2; 30, 3; iv. 6, 3, etc.; taittirīya Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 8, 5, etc.\nMaitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 9, 19; taittirīya Āraṇyaka, iv. 29.\nii. 438 (Journal of the American Oriental Society, 19, 101).\nx. 144, 4.\nii. 42, 2.\nAltindisches Leben, 88.\nIn the post-Vedic period suparṇa became a mythical bird, identified with Viṣnu's vehicle, Garuḍa, who, however, is also regarded as king of the Suparṇas.\nii. 30, 3.\nv. 4, 2.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["parNa", "parṇa", "su-parNa", "su-parNa_1", "su-parṇa", "su-parṇa_1", "suparNa", "suparṇa", "पर्ण", "सु-पर्ण", "सुपर्ण", "పర్ణ", "సు-పర్ణ", "సు-పర్ణ_౧", "సుపర్ణ", "ಪರ್ಣ", "ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ", "ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ_೧", "ಸುಪರ್ಣ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["parNa", "parṇa", "su-parNa", "su-parNa_1", "su-parṇa", "su-parṇa_1", "suparNa", "suparṇa", "parRa", "su-parRa", "suparRa", "పర్ణ", "సు-పర్ణ", "సు-పర్ణ_౧", "సుపర్ణ", "ಪರ್ಣ", "ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ", "ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ_೧", "ಸುಪರ್ಣ"]}], "su-pitrya": [{"id": "su-pitrya", "headword": "सु-पित्र्य", "headword_slp1": "su-pitrya", "fold": "su-pitrya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-pitrya, a word occurring once in the Rigveda, is probably an adjective (‘maintaining his paternal character well’). Ludwig, however, regards it, but without any great probability, as a proper name.\nFoot notes x. 115, 6.\nTranslation of the Rigveda, 3, 169.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["pitrya", "su-pitrya", "supitrya", "पित्र्य", "सुपित्र्य", "పిత్ర్య", "సు-పిత్ర్య", "సుపిత్ర్య", "ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ", "ಸುಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["pitrya", "su-pitrya", "supitrya", "pitrya", "supitrya", "పిత్ర్య", "సు-పిత్ర్య", "సుపిత్ర్య", "ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ", "ಸುಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ"]}], "su-pratita OluRqya": [{"id": "su-pratIta OluRqya", "headword": "सु-प्रतीत औलुण्ड्य", "headword_slp1": "su-pratIta OluRqya", "fold": "su-pratita OluRqya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-pratīta Auluṇḍya is the name of a teacher, a pupil of Bṛhaspatigupta, in the vaṃśa brāhmaṇa.\nFoot notes Indische Studien, 4, 372.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["prateeta auluNDya", "pratiita auluNDya", "pratīta auluṇḍya", "su-prateeta auluNDya", "su-pratiita auluNDya", "su-pratīta auluṇḍya", "suprateeta auluNDya", "supratiita auluNDya", "supratīta auluṇḍya", "प्रतीत औलुण्ड्य", "सुप्रतीत औलुण्ड्य", "ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "సు-ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "సుప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ", "ಸುಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["prateeta auluNDya", "pratiita auluNDya", "pratīta auluṇḍya", "su-prateeta auluNDya", "su-pratiita auluNDya", "su-pratīta auluṇḍya", "suprateeta auluNDya", "supratiita auluNDya", "supratīta auluṇḍya", "pratIta OluRqya", "supratIta OluRqya", "ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "సు-ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "సుప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య", "ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ", "ಸುಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ"]}], "su-banDu": [{"id": "su-banDu", "headword": "सु-बन्धु", "headword_slp1": "su-banDu", "fold": "su-banDu", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-bandhu in the hymns of the Rigveda is taken by Sāyaṇa to be a proper name; but this is not certain, Roth seeing in the passages only an ordinary noun meaning ‘a good friend.’ The later tradition explains that subandhu and his brothers, called Gaupāyanas, were priests of Asamāti, who cast them off and took two others, kirāta and ākuli. By these two in pigeon form subandhu was caused to swoon, but was revived by his three brothers, who recited certain hymns.\nFoot notes x. 59, 8; 60, 7, 10.\nSt. Petersburg Dictionary, s.v. 1.\nBṛhaddevatā, vii. 83 et seq., with Macdonell's notes. See also Asamāti, n. 1.\nRv. x. 57-60.\nCf. Max Müller, Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society, 2, 420-455; Oldenberg, Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft, 39, 90.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["bandhu", "su-bandhu", "subandhu", "बन्धु", "सुबन्धु", "బన్ధు", "సు-బన్ధు", "సుబన్ధు", "ಬನ್ಧು", "ಸು-ಬನ್ಧು", "ಸುಬನ್ಧು"], "synonyms_slp1": ["bandhu", "su-bandhu", "subandhu", "banDu", "subanDu", "బన్ధు", "సు-బన్ధు", "సుబన్ధు", "ಬನ್ಧು", "ಸು-ಬನ್ಧು", "ಸುಬನ್ಧು"]}], "su-brahmaRya": [{"id": "su-brahmaRya", "headword": "सु-ब्रह्मण्य", "headword_slp1": "su-brahmaRya", "fold": "su-brahmaRya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-brahmaṇya in the Brāhmaṇas denotes a priest who officiates as one of the three assistants of the Udgātṛ (see ṛtvij). His office is Subrahmaṇyā.\nFoot notes pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa, xxv. 4, 6; 18, 4; Śatapatha brāhmaṇa, iii. 3, 4, 9. Cf. Weber, Indische Studien, 10, 362, 374.\naitareya brāhmaṇa, vi. 3, 1-7. 11, 12; kauṣītaki brāhmaṇa, xxvii. 6, etc. The priest himself is so styled, aitareya brāhmaṇa, vii. 1, 2; pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa, xviii. 9, 19, etc.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["brahmaNya", "brahmaṇya", "su-brahmaNya", "su-brahmaṇya", "subrahmaNya", "subrahmaṇya", "ब्रह्मण्य", "सुब्रह्मण्य", "బ్రహ్మణ్య", "సు-బ్రహ్మణ్య", "సుబ్రహ్మణ్య", "ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ", "ಸುಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["brahmaNya", "brahmaṇya", "su-brahmaNya", "su-brahmaṇya", "subrahmaNya", "subrahmaṇya", "brahmaRya", "subrahmaRya", "బ్రహ్మణ్య", "సు-బ్రహ్మణ్య", "సుబ్రహ్మణ్య", "ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ", "ಸು-ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ", "ಸುಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ"]}], "su-Baga": [{"id": "su-BagA", "headword": "सु-भगा", "headword_slp1": "su-BagA", "fold": "su-Baga", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-bhagā, in the vocative subhage, is a frequent form of courteous address to women from the Rigveda onwards.\nFoot notes Rv. x. 10, 10. 12; 108, 5; Av. v. 5, 6; vi. 30, 3, etc.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["bhagaa", "bhagā", "su-bhagaa", "su-bhagā", "subhagaa", "subhagā", "भगा", "सुभगा", "భగా", "సు-భగా", "సుభగా", "ಭಗಾ", "ಸು-ಭಗಾ", "ಸುಭಗಾ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["bhagaa", "bhagā", "su-bhagaa", "su-bhagā", "subhagaa", "subhagā", "BagA", "suBagA", "భగా", "సు-భగా", "సుభగా", "ಭಗಾ", "ಸು-ಭಗಾ", "ಸುಭಗಾ"]}], "su-Badrika": [{"id": "su-BadrikA", "headword": "सु-भद्रिका", "headword_slp1": "su-BadrikA", "fold": "su-Badrika", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-bhadrikā occurs in the aśvamedha (‘horse sacrifice’) section of the yajurveda as in some way connected with the rite. Weber thinks that a proper name, that of the wife of the king of kāmpīla, is intended, but Mahīdhara explains the word merely as a lady with many lovers or a courtezan, a view followed by Roth. Since the taittirīya and kāṭhaka Saṃhitās have no subhadrikā, but a vocative subhage (see subhagā), the sense remains very doubtful.\nFoot notes Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 18 (cf. Satapatha brāhmaṇa, xiii. 2, 8, 3); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 20.\nIndische Studien, 1, 183, 184; Indian Literature, 114, 115. Cf. Griffith, Translation of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, 212, n.\nOn Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, loc. cit.\nSt. Petersburg Dictionary, s.v. subhadraka, 2b; Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, s.v. 2a.\nvii. 4, 19, 1; taittirīya brāhmaṇa, iii. 9, 6.\naśvamedha, iv. 8.\nCf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 36, 37; Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East, 44, 321, 322.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["bhadrikaa",