though Roth sees in the word the designation of a soma vessel. Its identification is quite uncertain, though it has been thought to be the of Megasthenes, the modern Suwan.\nFoot notes x. 75, 5; nirukta, ix. 26, where it is absurdly identified with the Sindu (Indus).\nviii. 7, 29.\nviii. 64, 11.\nSt. Petersburg Dictionary, s.v. 2.\nSee Arrian, Indica, iv. 12; Schwanbeck, Megasthenes, 31, where there is a various reading .\nCf. Hillebrandt, Vedische Mythologie, 1, 126 et seq.; Max Müller, Sacred Books of the East, 32, 398, 399; Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 12-14.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["Shomaa", "su-Shomaa", "su-ṣomā", "suShomaa", "suṣomā", "षोमा", "सुषोमा", "షోమా", "సు-షోమా", "సుషోమా", "ಷೋಮಾ", "ಸು-ಷೋಮಾ", "ಸುಷೋಮಾ", "ṣomā"], "synonyms_slp1": ["Shomaa", "su-Shomaa", "su-ṣomā", "suShomaa", "suṣomā", "zomA", "suzomA", "షోమా", "సు-షోమా", "సుషోమా", "ಷೋಮಾ", "ಸು-ಷೋಮಾ", "ಸುಷೋಮಾ", "ṣomā"]}], "su-sartu": [{"id": "su-sartu", "headword": "सु-सर्तु", "headword_slp1": "su-sartu", "fold": "su-sartu", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-sartu is the name of a river in the nadī-stuti (‘praise of rivers’) in the Rigveda. That it was a tributary of the Indus is certain, but which one is unknown.\nFoot notes x. 75, 6. Cf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 14; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["sartu", "su-sartu", "susartu", "सर्तु", "सुसर्तु", "సర్తు", "సు-సర్తు", "సుసర్తు", "ಸರ್ತು", "ಸು-ಸರ್ತು", "ಸುಸರ್ತು"], "synonyms_slp1": ["sartu", "su-sartu", "susartu", "sartu", "susartu", "సర్తు", "సు-సర్తు", "సుసర్తు", "ಸರ್ತು", "ಸು-ಸರ್ತು", "ಸುಸರ್ತು"]}], "su-havis angirasa": [{"id": "su-havis AMgirasa", "headword": "सु-हविस् आंगिरस", "headword_slp1": "su-havis AMgirasa", "fold": "su-havis angirasa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Su-havis Āṅgirasa (‘descendant of aṅgiras’) is the name of the seer of a sāman or chant in the pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa (xiv. 3, 25).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["havis aaMgirasa", "havis aangirasa", "havis āṅgirasa", "su-havis aaMgirasa", "su-havis aangirasa", "su-havis āṅgirasa", "suhavis aaMgirasa", "suhavis aangirasa", "suhavis āṅgirasa", "सु-हविस् आङ्गिरस", "सुहविस् आंगिरस", "सुहविस् आङ्गिरस", "हविस् आंगिरस", "हविस् आङ्गिरस", "సు-హవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "సు-హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "సుహవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "సుహవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "హవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ", "ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ", "ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["havis aaMgirasa", "havis aangirasa", "havis āṅgirasa", "su-havis aaMgirasa", "su-havis aangirasa", "su-havis āṅgirasa", "suhavis aaMgirasa", "suhavis aangirasa", "suhavis āṅgirasa", "su-havis ANgirasa", "suhavis AMgirasa", "suhavis ANgirasa", "havis AMgirasa", "havis ANgirasa", "సు-హవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "సు-హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "సుహవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "సుహవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "హవిస్ ఆంగిరస", "హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస", "ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ", "ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ", "ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ", "ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ"]}], "su-kara": [{"id": "sU-kara", "headword": "सू-कर", "headword_slp1": "sU-kara", "fold": "su-kara", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Sū-kara ‘wild-boar,’ has the appearance of being an onomatopoetic word (‘making the sound sū’); it is more probably a very old word going back to the Indo-European period, and cognate with the Latin su-culus (‘little pig’), being transformed in sense by popular etymology. It occurs in the Rigveda and later. It appears once in the Atharvaveda accompanied by mṛga, the combined words apparently meaning ‘wild hog,’ as opposed to varāha, ‘boar.’\nFoot notes The sū- corresponding to Lat. sū-s, Gk. -s, Old High German, sū. Cf. Brugmann, Grundriss, 2^2, 483.\nvii. 55, 4.\nAv. ii. 27, 2; v. 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40; Chāndogya upaniṣad, v. 10, 2, etc. None of the passages refer to the flesh as eaten: Buddha's death was due to a meal of sūkaramaddava, which may well mean ‘tender parts of pork’ (see Fleet, Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society, 1906, 881, n.), though the Rājanighaṇṭu, vii. 85, gives sūkara as meaning the Batatas edulis.\nxii. 1, 48. The use of mṛga here does not indicate that sūkara is a new name, because the latter word elsewhere always occurs alone both in the Rigveda and later (n. 3). Cf. mṛga above, 2, 172, n. 3.\nCf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 82; Pischel, Vedische Studien, 1, 100.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kara", "soo-kara", "sookara", "suu-kara", "suukara", "sū-kara", "sūkara", "कर", "सूकर", "కర", "సూ-కర", "సూకర", "ಕರ", "ಸೂ-ಕರ", "ಸೂಕರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kara", "soo-kara", "sookara", "suu-kara", "suukara", "sū-kara", "sūkara", "kara", "sUkara", "కర", "సూ-కర", "సూకర", "ಕರ", "ಸೂ-ಕರ", "ಸೂಕರ"]}]}